WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.350
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book two.

2
00:00:05.650 --> 00:00:09.211
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:16.563
Unit twenty-one: Philosophy of Mind. Today you will reflect on mind and brain using as if / as though hypotheticals.

4
00:00:16.863 --> 00:00:19.539
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:23.861
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.717 --> 00:00:25.625
Mind.

7
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:30.427
Mind means the faculty of thinking, feeling and awareness.

8
00:00:31.017 --> 00:00:33.515
The mind seems more than a heap of neurons.

9
00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:36.165
Your turn. Say: Mind.

10
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:39.885
Mind.

11
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:41.918
Consciousness.

12
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:47.029
Consciousness means the state of being aware of oneself and one's surroundings.

13
00:00:47.617 --> 00:00:50.382
Consciousness resists simple definition.

14
00:00:50.983 --> 00:00:54.135
Your turn. Say: Consciousness.

15
00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:58.094
Consciousness.

16
00:00:58.850 --> 00:00:59.770
Dualism.

17
00:01:00.783 --> 00:01:04.884
Dualism means the view that mind and body are two distinct kinds of thing.

18
00:01:05.483 --> 00:01:08.238
Descartes defended dualism of mind and body.

19
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:11.015
Your turn. Say: Dualism.

20
00:01:13.867 --> 00:01:14.903
Dualism.

21
00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:16.941
Materialism.

22
00:01:17.950 --> 00:01:22.260
Materialism means the view that everything, including mind, is physical.

23
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.289
Materialism holds that mental states are brain states.

24
00:01:26.883 --> 00:01:29.489
Your turn. Say: Materialism.

25
00:01:32.333 --> 00:01:33.671
Materialism.

26
00:01:34.417 --> 00:01:35.116
Brain.

27
00:01:36.117 --> 00:01:39.637
Brain means the organ that controls the body and gives rise to thought.

28
00:01:40.250 --> 00:01:42.923
The brain consumes a fifth of the body's energy.

29
00:01:43.517 --> 00:01:45.890
Your turn. Say: Brain.

30
00:01:48.733 --> 00:01:49.514
Brain.

31
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.065
Qualia.

32
00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.176
Qualia means the felt quality of experiences.

33
00:01:55.783 --> 00:01:58.921
Qualia seem hard to fit into a purely physical account.

34
00:01:59.517 --> 00:02:01.716
Your turn. Say: Qualia.

35
00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:05.498
Qualia.

36
00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:07.681
Intentionality.

37
00:02:08.683 --> 00:02:12.030
Intentionality means the aboutness of mental states.

38
00:02:12.617 --> 00:02:15.731
Beliefs have intentionality because they are about things.

39
00:02:16.317 --> 00:02:18.899
Your turn. Say: Intentionality.

40
00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:23.106
Intentionality.

41
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:25.002
Identity.

42
00:02:26.017 --> 00:02:29.502
Identity means what makes a person or thing the same over time.

43
00:02:30.117 --> 00:02:33.637
Personal identity persists despite constant bodily change.

44
00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:36.380
Your turn. Say: Identity.

45
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:40.258
Identity.

46
00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:44.520
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:45.383 --> 00:02:49.577
Visal, when I imagine my own thoughts, it feels as though they float above my body.

48
00:02:50.683 --> 00:02:58.595
That is the dualist intuition. It seems as if consciousness were more than chemistry, even if science says otherwise.

49
00:02:59.683 --> 00:03:02.333
So are materialists simply denying that feeling?

50
00:03:03.417 --> 00:03:12.002
Some behave as though qualia were illusions. Others admit that the feeling is real but insist it has a physical basis.

51
00:03:13.117 --> 00:03:14.373
And what do you think?

52
00:03:15.483 --> 00:03:26.971
I am agnostic. We talk as if we understood the relation of mind and brain, but we speak as though a final answer were already in hand, and it isn't.

53
00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:29.622
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:30.483 --> 00:03:34.352
Grammar focus: Hypothetical subjunctive (as if and as though).

55
00:03:34.652 --> 00:03:35.932
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:36.617 --> 00:03:39.650
It seems as if consciousness were more than chemistry.

57
00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.246
Some behave as though qualia were illusions.

58
00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:46.579
She speaks as if the answer were already known.

59
00:03:47.533 --> 00:03:57.326
After 'as if / as though' use the past (and 'were' for all persons) when the comparison is unreal or doubtful: as if he were. If the idea is likely true, the present is possible.

60
00:03:58.083 --> 00:04:02.942
Be careful! Don't say: It seems as if consciousness was more than chemistry.

61
00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:08.873
Say: It seems as if consciousness were more than chemistry.

62
00:04:09.173 --> 00:04:11.208
Use 'were' after as if (unreal).

63
00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.745
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:15.533 --> 00:04:16.720
Mind and Matter.

65
00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:23.571
The mind-body problem asks how mental events, such as pain, belief or intention, relate to the physical brain.

66
00:04:24.567 --> 00:04:32.093
Descartes proposed dualism, the claim that mind and matter are distinct substances, though he struggled to explain how they interact.

67
00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:41.032
Modern materialists reply that mental states are brain states, even if it seems as though experience were something over and above neural activity.

68
00:04:42.033 --> 00:04:48.839
The problem of qualia keeps the debate alive, for it is difficult to see how physical description could capture what a colour is like.

69
00:04:49.833 --> 00:04:56.198
Whatever the verdict, philosophers agree that the question of personal identity depends on how mind and brain are related.

70
00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.371
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:02.217 --> 00:05:05.226
It seems as if consciousness blank more than chemistry.

72
00:05:05.526 --> 00:05:08.704
A, is being. B, were. C, will.

73
00:05:12.267 --> 00:05:13.988
The answer is B: were.

74
00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.626
The view that mind and body are distinct is blank.

75
00:05:17.926 --> 00:05:21.939
A, materialism. B, dualism. C, identity.

76
00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.476
The answer is B: dualism.

77
00:05:28.617 --> 00:05:31.115
The view that everything is physical is blank.

78
00:05:31.415 --> 00:05:35.394
A, materialism. B, qualia. C, dualism.

79
00:05:38.933 --> 00:05:40.863
The answer is A: materialism.

80
00:05:42.017 --> 00:05:43.645
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:43.945 --> 00:05:51.754
A, She talks as if she was an expert.. B, She talks as if she were an expert.. C, She talks as if she be an expert..

82
00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.623
The answer is B: She talks as if she were an expert..

83
00:05:59.783 --> 00:06:02.747
According to the reading, the problem of qualia is that…

84
00:06:03.047 --> 00:06:09.881
A, colour is unreal. B, physical description struggles to capture experience. C, brains are simple.

85
00:06:13.433 --> 00:06:18.254
The answer is B: physical description struggles to capture experience.

86
00:06:19.467 --> 00:06:22.769
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:23.617 --> 00:06:25.454
Is the mind the same as the brain?

88
00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:43.727
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:44.027 --> 00:07:12.064
I think the mind depends entirely on the brain, yet it may not be fully reducible to it. Damage to the brain changes personality and memory, so the dependence is clear. Still, it seems as if experience were more than a list of neural events, because we cannot easily describe what a feeling is like in physical terms. I therefore see the mind as arising from the brain without being reduced to it.

90
00:07:12.364 --> 00:07:13.992
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:14.883 --> 00:07:20.101
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and hypothetical subjunctive (as if and as though).

92
00:07:20.401 --> 00:07:25.643
Today's value is open-mindedness. Be open to new places, people and ideas.

93
00:07:25.943 --> 00:07:28.642
See you in Unit twenty-two. Goodbye!

94
00:07:28.942 --> 00:07:31.466
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

